<ResourceDictionary xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml/presentation"
                    xmlns:x="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml">

    <!--
        THE CONTROLS THIS WINDOW IS MADE OF, EVERYWHERE EXCEPT THE LIST - the text styles,
        the sentences the window says, the field somebody types into and the box somebody
        ticks. Not one value of its own: every number, colour and thickness below is a name
        declared in Values.xaml.

        ADR-23, the same rule as the other two files.

        THIS FILE SHED THE LIST ON 2026-08-11 AND KEPT ITS NAME, because the name was always
        describing what is still here. Themes/List.xaml took the grid, the row template, the
        cell, the headings and the marks, unchanged and in the order they were in. It went
        because this file stood exactly on its 530-line ceiling and the configurable columns
        of S6d2 needed cell templates a resource dictionary could hand to a column built in
        code.

        THE media PREFIX WENT WITH THE LIST, and nothing beyond x is declared here on
        purpose. The mutation entry that proves a styles file holds no value has to inject a
        type the file can actually parse, so it uses Thickness rather than system:Double -
        which only works while neither styles file declares the system prefix.

        NEITHER THIS FILE NOR List.xaml LOADS ON ITS OWN. Both reach WPF UI's keys and both
        reach ours in Values.xaml, and StaticResource resolves them WHILE THE FILE IS BEING
        READ. So the merge order in App.xaml is the library's dictionaries, then Values.xaml,
        then this, then List.xaml.
    -->
    <!--
        BasedOn by NAME on WPF UI's own styles, not on {x:Type TextBlock}.

        The type form worked while the base was the built-in Fluent theme, and would now be
        ambiguous: this dictionary is merged after theirs and is itself keyed by the same type,
        so which style it resolves to depends on lookup order rather than on intent. Their keys
        are stable, documented by their own markup - DataGrid.xaml bases four styles on exactly
        these names - and they say what is meant.

        CONSEQUENCE TO KNOW: this file no longer loads on its own. It resolves keys that only
        exist once ControlsDictionary is merged, which is why tools/wpfui-probe loads it after
        theirs. That is a real dependency and it is the shape of the decision the owner made.

        Every one of the three below keeps OUR size setter on top of their template. That is
        the density decision of 2026-08-02 in the two places it is spelled out.
    -->
    <Style TargetType="TextBlock" BasedOn="{StaticResource BodyTextBlockStyle}">
        <Setter Property="FontFamily" Value="{StaticResource TextFace}" />
        <Setter Property="FontSize" Value="{StaticResource TextSizeNormal}" />
    </Style>

    <Style x:Key="HeadingText" TargetType="TextBlock">
        <Setter Property="FontFamily" Value="{StaticResource TextFace}" />
        <Setter Property="FontSize" Value="{StaticResource TextSizeHeading}" />
    </Style>

    <Style x:Key="FixedText" TargetType="TextBlock">
        <Setter Property="FontFamily" Value="{StaticResource TextFaceFixed}" />
        <Setter Property="FontSize" Value="{StaticResource TextSizeNormal}" />
    </Style>

    <Style x:Key="SubduedText" TargetType="TextBlock">
        <Setter Property="FontFamily" Value="{StaticResource TextFace}" />
        <Setter Property="FontSize" Value="{StaticResource TextSizeSmall}" />
        <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{StaticResource TextSubdued}" />
    </Style>

    <!--
        THE COUNT, WHICH MOVED OUT FROM UNDER THE LIST AND STOPPED BEING SUBDUED. Complaint 8 of
        the eleven says it was the least visible thing on the screen and is the main answer to
        every keystroke - small, grey and at the bottom is three ways of saying "ignore me".

        SEMIBOLD WHEN THE LIST IS NARROWED, AND THAT IS COMPLAINT 7 RATHER THAN DECORATION. That
        one says you cannot see THAT a filter is on. The words already say it - "108 of 810"
        against "810" - but a weight change is seen without reading, which is the difference
        between a signal and a fact you have to go and check.

        TextSizeHeader rather than TextSizeHeading: 14 is the floor for semibold text in the
        guidance TypeScaleGuards holds, and this line is semibold half the time.
    -->
    <Style x:Key="CountLine" TargetType="TextBlock">
        <Setter Property="FontFamily" Value="{StaticResource TextFace}" />
        <Setter Property="FontSize" Value="{StaticResource TextSizeHeader}" />
        <Setter Property="FontWeight" Value="{StaticResource TextWeightNormal}" />

        <!--
            Missing until 2026-08-10, and the count was black on a dark window. A regression from
            slice 5, where it "stopped being grey" - the brush was removed rather than replaced,
            so this style declared type and no colour and inherited one nobody chose. Backlog 145.
        -->
        <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{StaticResource TextOnSurface}" />
        <Style.Triggers>
            <DataTrigger Binding="{Binding Says.Narrowed}" Value="True">
                <Setter Property="FontWeight" Value="{StaticResource TextWeightHeader}" />
            </DataTrigger>
        </Style.Triggers>
    </Style>

    <!--
        The sentence in the middle of an empty list. Larger than the rows, because it is the only
        thing on that half of the screen and somebody is looking at it wondering what happened.
    -->
    <Style x:Key="EmptyStateText" TargetType="TextBlock">
        <Setter Property="FontFamily" Value="{StaticResource TextFace}" />
        <Setter Property="FontSize" Value="{StaticResource TextSizeHeading}" />
        <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{StaticResource TextSubdued}" />
    </Style>

    <!--
        What the result has to admit about itself, and what is wrong with the question. Two
        styles rather than one, because they answer different questions and a person glancing
        at the window has to be able to tell "your query is broken" from "your query worked and
        the answer has holes in it" without reading either sentence.
    -->
    <Style x:Key="NoticeText" TargetType="TextBlock">
        <Setter Property="FontFamily" Value="{StaticResource TextFace}" />
        <Setter Property="FontSize" Value="{StaticResource TextSizeSmall}" />
        <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{StaticResource MeaningWarning}" />
    </Style>

    <Style x:Key="ProblemText" TargetType="TextBlock">
        <Setter Property="FontFamily" Value="{StaticResource TextFace}" />
        <Setter Property="FontSize" Value="{StaticResource TextSizeSmall}" />
        <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{StaticResource MeaningRejected}" />
    </Style>

    <!--
        The one field. Its padding is named rather than left to the framework because the hint
        sitting behind it has to line up with the text a person types, and the only way to be
        sure of that is for both to come from this line.

        BasedOn is the whole reason this comment is long. Without it a default style here does
        not ADD to the base one, it REPLACES it - so the field came out white, with grey hint
        text, on a dark window. It shipped that way from S6a until 2026-08-02, through a
        screenshot review that had the field on screen.

        Found by accident, while photographing this window to compare it against a control
        library. That is the fourth time in this project that XAML has bound correctly and
        painted something other than what it promised, after the empty list from internal view
        models, the column headers outside the visual tree, and the dead DataGridRow background.
    -->
    <Style TargetType="TextBox" BasedOn="{StaticResource DefaultTextBoxStyle}">
        <Setter Property="Padding" Value="{StaticResource PadCell}" />
        <Setter Property="FontFamily" Value="{StaticResource TextFace}" />
        <Setter Property="FontSize" Value="{StaticResource TextSizeNormal}" />
    </Style>

    <Style TargetType="CheckBox" BasedOn="{StaticResource DefaultCheckBoxStyle}">
        <Setter Property="FontFamily" Value="{StaticResource TextFace}" />
        <Setter Property="FontSize" Value="{StaticResource TextSizeNormal}" />
    </Style>

    <!--
        ONE COLUMN IN THE LIST OF COLUMNS SOMEBODY CHOOSES FROM - `A8`, arriving 2026-08-11.

        A MENU RATHER THAN A PANEL OF OUR OWN, AND THAT IS THE CHEAPEST HONEST SURFACE IN THIS
        WINDOW. A floating panel needs a fill, an edge and both of their contrast ratios argued in
        ContrastGuards, for a thing that is on screen while somebody clicks four times a year. A
        menu is already themed by the control library, is already used by this window for copying a
        name, and was already looked at by a person on the release build. Nothing new is declared
        here and no colour is invented.

        BasedOn OR THE LIBRARY IS INSTALLED AND INVISIBLE FOR THIS CONTROL - `docs/10` trap 4. An
        explicit style REPLACES an implicit one rather than adding to it, so without this line the
        items would come out in the built-in Windows look inside a Fluent window.

        BasedOn ON THE TYPE RATHER THAN ON A NAME, AND THAT IS MEASURED. Every other BasedOn in
        this product names one of their keys, so this one was written DefaultMenuItemStyle by
        analogy - and there is no such key. WindowGuards went red at once with the resource name in
        the message, which is the whole reason that guard builds a window rather than trusting a
        compile. Their assembly declares DefaultMenuStyle, DefaultToolTipStyle, DefaultButtonStyle
        and eighty others, and no named style for a menu ITEM: it is registered against the type.
        So the base is asked for the same way the framework asks for it.
    -->
    <!--
        WHAT THIS COSTS AND WHY IT IS ACCEPTED: an implicit style is looked up by type at merge
        time, so this line goes quiet rather than red if the library ever stops registering one.
        A named key throws. Nothing here can be named, so the honest arrangement is to say so.

        StaysOpenOnClick, because turning on four columns is one errand rather than four. A menu
        that shuts after every tick makes a person open it four times to do one thing.

        Not one appearance value here, which is why this style is allowed to say nothing about how
        it looks: what a column is CALLED, whether it is on, and whether it may be turned off are
        all answered by the choice it is bound to.
    -->
    <Style x:Key="ColumnChoiceItem" TargetType="MenuItem" BasedOn="{StaticResource {x:Type MenuItem}}">
        <Setter Property="Header" Value="{Binding Label}" />
        <Setter Property="IsCheckable" Value="True" />
        <Setter Property="IsChecked" Value="{Binding IsShown, Mode=TwoWay}" />
        <Setter Property="IsEnabled" Value="{Binding MayHide}" />
        <Setter Property="StaysOpenOnClick" Value="True" />
    </Style>
</ResourceDictionary>
